  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "corpus-crlf-not-byte-identical",
      "boundaryId": "sprint-1",
      "criterionIds": [
        "corpus.vendored"
      ],
      "severity": "major",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** the vendored `2025.10/` tree is an unmodified copy of upstream `test-suite/tests/2025.10/` at `882ebd6` (decision `corpus-ratchet`: fixture files are never edited), and the README claims `.gitattributes` keeps the files byte-identical.\n\n**Observed:** all 253 vendored files (3 manifests + 250 fixtures) have CRLF line endings in the working tree; upstream blobs are LF. Because `.gitattributes` sets `* -text`, `git add` stores the CRLF bytes as-is: `git hash-object` of `resolver/positive/minimal-resolver.json` gives `ff38d65a...`, while the upstream tree lists `b76e83e6...`. None of the 253 files match their upstream blob SHA; after stripping CR bytes, all 253 match and the file set is identical. The parse-based harness cannot detect this.\n\n**Repair:** re-export the tree byte-exact (for example `git archive 882ebd6 test-suite/tests/2025.10 | tar -x` with no autocrlf conversion, or convert CRLF to LF), keep `* -text`, and verify each file's `git hash-object --no-filters` against the upstream tree for `882ebd6`. Add a cheap guard to the `official_test_suite` group (for example, assert that no manifest or fixture contains a CR byte) so the automated criterion covers byte identity.\n\n**Resolved:** re-exported with `core.autocrlf=false`; `git hash-object` of all 253 files equals the upstream blob ids at `882ebd6` (re-verified by the reviewer). The README update procedure disables autocrlf and requires blob-id verification, and `vendored_fixtures_keep_the_upstream_line_endings` fails on any CR byte or a changed file count.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "dtcg",
          "path": "crates/dtcg_runtime/tests/official_test_suite.rs",
          "symbol": "vendored_fixtures_keep_the_upstream_line_endings"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "schema-root-inline-preview-source",
      "boundaryId": "sprint-1",
      "criterionIds": [
        "format.schema-root"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** only whole-document sources may carry a root `$schema`; fragment roots and inline sources do not qualify (design `format-structure`, and the doc comment on `without_root_schema`), so `$schema` there fails with `unknown reserved property`.\n\n**Observed:** `without_root_schema` (resolver.rs) only checks `source.origin.pointer.is_empty()`. The inline source supplied to `inspect_source`/`preview_source` is built in `discover_source` (source_discovery.rs) with `origin { resource: None, pointer: \"\" }`, so it is treated as a document root. Probe: `runtime.preview_source(&json!({\"$schema\":\"x\",\"b\":{\"$type\":\"number\",\"$value\":2}}), &[])` returns `Ok(Ready(..))` with 2 tokens instead of a `Token` error. The existing inline test only covers inline sources inside a resolver `sources` array (non-empty pointer).\n\n**Repair:** treat a source as a document root only when it was loaded as a whole document (for example `source.origin.resource.is_some() && source.origin.pointer.is_empty()`), and add a `preview_source` inline case to `schema_in_fragment_and_inline_sources_is_not_a_document_root`.\n\n**Resolved:** `without_root_schema` now requires `origin.resource.is_some()` and an empty pointer, and `schema_in_fragment_and_inline_sources_is_not_a_document_root` asserts that `preview_source` with an inline root `$schema` fails with `unknown reserved property`.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "dtcg",
          "path": "crates/dtcg_runtime/tests/official_test_suite.rs",
          "symbol": "schema_in_fragment_and_inline_sources_is_not_a_document_root"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "api-docs-missing-or-inaccurate",
      "boundaryId": "sprint-1",
      "criterionIds": [
        "api.docs"
      ],
      "severity": "minor",
      "status": "resolved",
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** every public item touched by this epic has a doc comment describing its current behavior.\n\n**Observed (resolver.rs):**\n- New public `Resolution::is_ready`, `Resolution::is_missing`, and `RuntimeLimits::with_max_documents` / `with_max_reference_depth` / `with_max_alias_depth` have no doc comments.\n- The new `RuntimeLimits::max_documents` doc says \"Documents one discovery may load or request\", but `Discovery::check_count` bounds source occurrences (terminal and queued sources, inline ones included, plus missing requests), as user-guide chapter 11 states; a resolver with 300 inline sources and no external document exceeds the default.\n- The new `max_alias_depth` doc says \"Reference hops in one token's resolution chain\", but `resolve_token` rejects when `visiting.len() >= max_alias_depth` before visiting a token, so it bounds tokens on the chain (N hops need a limit of N+1, and `0` rejects literal tokens).\n\n**Repair:** add doc comments to the five new methods and rewrite the two field docs to describe the actual counting (and update them again if requirement `bounds` changes the semantics).\n\n**Resolved:** `is_ready`, `is_missing` and the three `with_*` setters are documented; `max_documents` now documents source-occurrence counting and `max_alias_depth` documents tokens on the chain including the referring token, matching `check_count` and `resolve_token`. Sprint 2 must update the alias-depth doc when it changes the semantics.",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "dtcg",
          "path": "crates/dtcg_runtime/src/resolver.rs",
          "symbol": "RuntimeLimits"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
